\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{figures/fig_framework.pdf}
\caption{The diminishing weakest link in LLM judge panels. \textbf{(a)}~In small panels ($K{=}3$), a single vulnerable judge (red) can flip the majority; individual weakness ($\eta_{\max}$) dominates vulnerability ($\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}{=}0.243$). \textbf{(b)}~As panel size grows, the balance shifts from individual weakness to collective diversity. \textbf{(c)}~In large panels ($K{\geq}5$), inter-judge diversity ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$, $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}{=}0.353$) drives vulnerability while $\eta_{\max}$ diminishes (CI crosses zero). Bottom bars show relative predictive weight.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

Multi-judge panels have become the dominant defense against bias and manipulation in Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation. After \citet{zheng2023judging} demonstrated that individual LLM judges can rival human annotators, subsequent work exposed systematic vulnerabilities: position bias, verbosity preferences, and sycophancy compromise the reliability of any single judge \citep{wang2023fair}. The natural response has been to aggregate decisions across diverse models, an approach inspired by Byzantine fault tolerance (BFT), where majority voting among independent nodes tolerates faulty responses \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}. A recent systematization reports that 7-model committees reduce attack success rates (ASR) from 50--68\% to 10--19\% \citep{almasoud2026security}. Diversity, by this logic, is the key ingredient for secure evaluation, yet this framing conflates two distinct sources of robustness: the quality of individual judges and the independence among them.

The conflation matters because the BFT analogy breaks down in the LLM judge setting. BFT adversaries control faulty nodes from within; evaluation adversaries manipulate inputs from without, and shared pretraining corpora create correlated failure modes that violate the independence assumption underlying majority-vote guarantees \citep{schneier2000secrets}. No existing work disentangles diversity from individual robustness across panel sizes, so the relative contribution of each factor to observed ASR reductions remains unclear.

We address this gap by decomposing panel vulnerability into two predictors (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}): weakest-link susceptibility ($\eta_{\max}$, the maximum individual attack success rate in a panel) and effective independent channels ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$, an information-theoretic measure of judge independence). Controlled experiments across 15 models (9 API, 6 local quantized), $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, six attack types, and two multiple-choice benchmarks (Massive Multitask Language Understanding [MMLU] and the AI2 Reasoning Challenge [ARC]) reveal two robust patterns and one metric-sensitive observation.

These findings yield three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Diversity positively associated with vulnerability.} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ confirms a positive association with panel attack success in 10 of 12 conditions (10/12 direction under OLS and generalized additive model bootstrap); $\kappa$-diversity confirms 12/12. The two exceptions---both position-bias conditions---show negative $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$, consistent with diversity providing genuine redundancy against shared-bias attacks (\S\ref{sec:gam_taxonomy}). However, the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) precludes causal interpretation: Cinelli--Hazlett robustness values do not exceed the confound in 69\% of conditions (\S\ref{sec:dominance}, \S\ref{sec:gam_taxonomy}).
\item \textbf{Diminishing weakest link.} $\eta_{\max}$'s predictive power declines monotonically with panel size: meta-analytic pooled $\hat{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}$ falls from $0.243$ [$0.163, 0.323$] at $K{=}3$ to $0.091$ [$-0.031, 0.212$] at $K{=}5$ to $0.086$ [$-0.039, 0.211$] at $K{=}7$; the confidence interval crosses zero at $K{\geq}5$. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ remains a consistently strong predictor (WCB significance 7--8/12 across all $K$), while $\eta_{\max}$ WCB significance drops from 5/12 ($K{=}3$) to 0/12 ($K{=}5$) to 1/12 ($K{=}7$). $\kappa$-diversity decreases ($8 \to 7 \to 6$/12), indicating that the $K$-dependent pattern is operationalization-sensitive (\S\ref{sec:cross_k}).
\item \textbf{Aggregation as predictability control.} Aggregation rules modulate how much variance panel composition explains ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09). Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), so the defense is partial (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

All findings are associational; the diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$; \S\ref{sec:analysis}) precludes causal claims.

